    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d756">

                <pb facs="0938" n="930" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg930"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">761.9411/73: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-04-05T09:00:00+07:00">April 5, 1941—9
                            [a.m.?]</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 5—6:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>127. The Foreign Minister<anchor n="40" xml:id="d756-f1"/> informed me yesterday
                    that he had received a “reliable but unconfirmed” report from Moscow to the
                    effect that when Matsuoka was recently received by Stalin and Molotov he
                    approached the latter and suggested the continuation of negotiations said to
                    have been initiated during the incumbency of Litvinov as Soviet Foreign Minister
                    for the conclusion of a nonaggression pact between Japan and the Soviet Union;
                    and that Matsuoka was informed in reply that while it is the policy of the
                    Soviet Union to negotiate nonaggression pacts with its neighbors in the case of
                    Japan it would be necessary to bring about a restoration of the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">status quo ante</hi> of the Portsmouth Treaty<anchor
                        n="41" xml:id="d756-f2"/> before the Soviet Union would consider the
                    conclusion of such a pact with Japan. The Foreign Minister, who seemed to think
                    that the report had some basis in fact, expressed the view that the Russian
                    counterproposal was tantamount to a “polite refusal” of Matsuoka’s proposal.</p>
                <p>Sent to the Department, repeated to Peking.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
